WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:12.050
In this video, you create the largest virtual machine a single ARM64 host in SUSE Virtualization accepts: 512 virtual CPUs and 160 gigabytes of memory.

00:00:12.050 --> 00:00:20.310
Each host in this cluster is an Ampere Altra server with one hundred sixty cores and about one hundred twenty gigabytes of allocatable memory.

00:00:20.310 --> 00:00:22.570
Go to Virtual Machines

00:00:22.570 --> 00:00:32.760
and select Create.

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:35.500
Choose the namespace

00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:37.150
and a name.

00:00:37.150 --> 00:00:44.940
Enter five hundred twelve CPUs. That is the hard ceiling of the ARM64 virt machine type, even on a host with fewer cores.

00:00:44.940 --> 00:01:03.080
Enter one hundred sixty gigabytes of memory, more than the host can allocate.

00:01:03.080 --> 00:01:04.920
On the Volumes tab,

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:10.420
select the openSUSE Leap ARM64 cloud image.

00:01:10.420 --> 00:01:12.460
On Advanced Options,

00:01:12.460 --> 00:01:18.080
select a cloud configuration template that creates a demo user with a console password.

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:21.710
Select Create.

00:01:21.710 --> 00:01:29.010
The virtual machine is running with five hundred twelve CPUs and one hundred sixty gigabytes, on a single host.

00:01:29.010 --> 00:01:32.650
The guest has finished booting. Open its console.

00:01:35.950 --> 00:01:38.340
Log in as the demo user

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:43.400
and the password from the template.

00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:55.230
Check the processor count and the memory.

00:01:55.230 --> 00:02:05.839
The host has only one hundred sixty cores, so these CPUs are overcommitted and suit testing more than production. Next, you pack many small virtual machines onto the cluster with overcommit.
